# Triaged gitleaks baseline (issue #98).
#
# Each line is a gitleaks FINGERPRINT: `commit:path:rule:line`. That shape is the
# reason this file is a baseline and not a hole in the gate. It pins a finding to one
# rule at one line of one commit, so a NEW secret — in the same file, even on the same
# line of a later commit — still fires. Do not replace these with a `[allowlist]`
# `paths` entry in a `.gitleaks.toml`: allowlisting `tests/` would blind the scanner to
# a real key committed into a test, which is a likely place for one to land.
#
# Every entry below was read before it was added. The scan that produced them ran over
# all 412 commits of this repository's history, not the working tree, and found exactly
# these 13. Adding a line here without reading the underlying value is how a baseline
# becomes the thing that hides the incident.
#
# ── tests/*.rs: one test-only RSA private key, generated for this suite ──────────────
# The same key literal appears in four test files as `TEST_RSA_PRIVATE_KEY` /
# `SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY`. It signs assertions against a JWKS the test itself publishes;
# it authenticates nothing outside the test process. `tests/secret_leak_snapshots.rs`
# goes further and uses it as a deliberate NEEDLE — that suite fails if the key or any
# signature made with it ever appears on a driven surface. Removing it from the repo
# would delete the fixture that proves Moira does not leak keys.
